    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This research explores the relationships and interactions between youth and authority in a Southern California community-centered, youth-focused restorative justice setting. Adult authority figures in this setting are educators, law enforcement, and restorative justice staff (i.e. case managers) who intervene and engage with youth after they have been arrested. Over a two year period, this research conducted ethnographic observations of restorative justice “contract-making processes” and semi-structured interviews with youth after this process. Through these methods, this research asks: First, how do youth perceive their interactions and experiences with adult authority figures in restorative justice processes? Secondly, what are the impacts of these perceptions on the functions and outcomes of restorative justice programs? Observations and interviews demonstrated a multitude of experiences with different actors, resulting in both positive and negative interactions. Differences in these experiences show how interactions can have a significant impact on how young people participate in the restorative justice process. This paper pushes literature on restorative justice work to look more closely into frequently overlooked micro-interactions as they provide crucial insights into how outcomes develop in this process.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Flores, J. (2024). Vetting Restorative Justice: Exploring Youth Perspectives, Interactions, and Processes In Alternative Diversion Court. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/ff108xk9709.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
